 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Probably not just you, but Perth's price rise has been much smaller than the national one.**\n\n- **Perth electricity prices** were up **2.4%** year-on-year in August 2026 (latest data). The index was 102.4 in August 2026, against 95.2 in June 2024.\n- **National electricity prices** were up **13.2%** year-on-year in August 2026, and up about 38% from the low point in October 2025 (index 89.8 to 113.7).\n\nSo the average Perth household's electricity price is not up sharply over the past year. The Perth series also swings a lot month to month. The big jumps in mid-2025 and early 2026 look like state rebate timing effects, but I can't confirm the exact cause from this data.\n\nA high bill can still come from factors the price index doesn't capture:\n- **Usage:** heating or cooling use is higher in winter and summer, and a hot or cold spell can add a lot.\n- **Rebates or credits:** if a state rebate ended or was reduced, the bill goes up even if the tariff hasn't changed.\n- **Tariff or plan changes:** a new plan, a changed supply charge, or a new tariff year (many change from 1 July) can raise the cost.\n- **Estimated meter reads:** if the retailer estimated your usage, a later actual read can produce a catch-up bill.\n\nTo check, compare your kWh usage and daily supply charge on this bill with the one from a year ago, and see whether any rebate appears on it. If the usage is similar and the charges are higher, ask your retailer for an itemised breakdown or the tariff they're applying. You can also compare plans on the Western Australian government's energy comparison site.",
